The command that does more work per line than almost anything else in Cypress — and the mechanism Part 0.5 promised would be uniquely precise because Cypress patches the network layer from inside the page itself.

## The mechanism, briefly revisited

Recall from Part 0.5: because Cypress test code shares a JS realm with your application, `cy.intercept()` works by monkey-patching `window.fetch` and `XMLHttpRequest.prototype` at the moment the app iframe loads — before your application's own JavaScript runs. This is why intercepts must be registered before the action that triggers the request (Part 3.11's recurring warning): if the patch isn't in place when your app's code calls fetch(), Cypress never sees that call.

```js
// ❌ Too late — the GET already fired before the intercept existed
cy.visit('/leave');
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests').as('getLeave');

// ✅ Intercept registered before the triggering action
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests').as('getLeave');
cy.visit('/leave');
cy.wait('@getLeave');
```

This ordering rule is the single most common `cy.intercept()` mistake, and it's worth internalizing as a reflex: intercepts go first, always.

## The three things cy.intercept() does

`cy.intercept()` serves three distinct purposes that are easy to conflate because they share one API surface:
- **Spying** — observe real requests without changing them, for waiting and assertion
- **Stubbing** — replace the real response with a fake one, controlling status, body, headers, delay
- **Modifying in-flight** — intercept a real request or response and alter it before it continues

## Basic syntax and route matching

```js
cy.intercept(method, url);
cy.intercept(method, url, response);
cy.intercept(routeMatcher);
cy.intercept(routeMatcher, response);
cy.intercept(method, url, handlerFunction);

// Method + exact path
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests').as('getLeave');

// Wildcards
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees/*').as('getEmployee');           // one path segment
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees/**').as('getEmployeeDeep');      // any depth
cy.intercept('GET', '**/leave-requests').as('getLeaveAnyHost');      // any origin/prefix

// Regex — for precise matching against variable query strings
cy.intercept('GET', /\/api\/leave-requests\?status=pending/).as('getPending');

// Route matcher object — the most flexible form
cy.intercept({
  method: 'GET',
  url: '/api/employees',
  query: { department: 'Engineering' },
}).as('getEngineers');

cy.intercept({
  method: 'POST',
  url: '/api/leave-requests',
  headers: { 'x-custom-header': 'test' },
}).as('createLeaveWithHeader');
```

A critical, easy-to-miss default: without a method, `cy.intercept()` matches GET requests only if you pass just a URL string — actually, more precisely, omitting the method matches any HTTP method against that URL:
`cy.intercept('/api/leave-requests')`.as('anyLeaveCall');   // matches GET, POST, PUT, DELETE — all of them

This is a common source of confusion: an alias intended to mean "the GET that loads the list" silently also catches the POST that creates a new one, and `cy.wait('@anyLeaveCall')` resolves on whichever fires first — not necessarily the one you meant. Always specify the method explicitly unless you deliberately want to match everything.

## Stubbing — the static response form

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', {
  statusCode: 200,
  body: [
    { id: 1, employeeName: 'Aarav Sharma', type: 'Sick', status: 'Pending' },
    { id: 2, employeeName: 'Bibek Thapa', type: 'Annual', status: 'Approved' },
  ],
}).as('getLeave');
```

Shorthand — passing just a body assumes a 200:

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', [
  { id: 1, employeeName: 'Aarav Sharma', status: 'Pending' },
]).as('getLeave');
```

Error responses, for testing failure UI that's otherwise nearly impossible to trigger on demand:

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', {
  statusCode: 500,
  body: { error: 'Internal Server Error' },
}).as('getLeaveError');

cy.visit('/leave');
cy.wait('@getLeaveError');
cy.get('[data-cy=error-banner]').should('contain', 'Something went wrong');
cy.get('[data-cy=retry-btn]').should('be.visible');

// Network failure simulation — no response at all
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', { forceNetworkError: true }).as('networkFail');
cy.get('[data-cy=offline-banner]').should('be.visible');
```

Headers on a stub:

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', {
  statusCode: 200,
  headers: { 'x-total-count': '47' },
  body: leaveRequests,
});
```

## Stubbing with fixtures — the standard idiom

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', { fixture: 'leave-requests.json' }).as('getLeave');

// Dynamic status alongside a fixture body
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', {
  statusCode: 200,
  fixture: 'leave-requests.json',
});

// Multiple fixtures for related endpoints
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees', { fixture: 'employees.json' });
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/departments', { fixture: 'departments.json' });
```

This is the pairing flagged back in Part 1.3 — fixtures aren't just test input data, they're the standard mock response body for `cy.intercept()`. A realistic project keeps a fixtures/ folder organized by endpoint, and this combination is how the overwhelming majority of "test the UI in isolation from the backend" scenarios get written.

## Dynamic responses — a handler function

For responses that need to vary based on the request itself:

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees/*', (req) => {
  const id = req.url.split('/').pop();
  req.reply({
    statusCode: 200,
    body: { id: Number(id), name: `Employee ${id}`, department: 'Engineering' },
  });
});

cy.intercept('POST', '/api/leave-requests', (req) => {
  req.reply({
    statusCode: 201,
    body: { id: Date.now(), ...req.body, status: 'Pending' },
  });
}).as('createLeave');

```

## req.reply() vs letting a real request through

If a handler function doesn't call `req.reply()`, the request passes through to the real server — this is what makes spying (observe only, don't touch) different from stubbing:

```js
// SPY — just observe, let the real backend respond
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests').as('getLeave');
cy.visit('/leave');
cy.wait('@getLeave').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);

// STUB — replace the response entirely
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', { fixture: 'leave-requests.json' }).as('getLeave');
```

## Modifying real requests and responses in-flight

This is the third category, and it's genuinely powerful — you're not replacing the network call, you're intercepting the real one and tweaking it:

```js
// Modify an outgoing request before it reaches the server
cy.intercept('POST', '/api/leave-requests', (req) => {
  req.headers['x-test-run'] = 'true';
  req.body.source = 'e2e-test';
  // no req.reply() → continues to the REAL server with the modified request
});

// Modify a real response after it comes back
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees', (req) => {
  req.continue((res) => {
    res.body.push({ id: 999, name: 'Injected Test Employee' });
  });
});

// Force a delay on a real response — this is how you test loading states reliably
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', (req) => {
  req.on('response', (res) => {
    res.setDelay(2000);
  });
}).as('slowLeave');

cy.visit('/leave');
cy.get('[data-cy=skeleton-loader]').should('be.visible');
cy.wait('@slowLeave');
cy.get('[data-cy=skeleton-loader]').should('not.exist');
```

This delay-injection pattern deserves special attention because it solves a real, otherwise-unsolvable problem: loading states on a fast local dev server or a well-cached staging environment often render and disappear in under 50ms — far too fast to reliably assert on with a normal `cy.get()`.should('be.visible'), which might run its first check after the spinner is already gone. Forcing a deliberate delay via `cy.intercept()` makes the loading state observable on demand, every time, regardless of how fast the real backend actually is. This is meaningfully better than the alternative of artificially slowing down your actual backend for testing purposes.
`req.continue()` also accepts no callback, simply passing the request through unmodified — useful when you only want to intercept for the purpose of aliasing/waiting without touching anything:

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', (req) => {
  req.continue();
}).as('getLeave');
```

Though in practice, this is identical to the simple `cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests')`.as('getLeave') spy form — the handler function is only needed when you're actually doing something with the request or response.

## Multiple intercepts, precedence, and route matching order

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees/*', { fixture: 'employee-default.json' });
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees/123', { fixture: 'employee-123-special.json' });
```

Cypress matches the most-recently-registered intercept first, and falls back to earlier ones for requests that don't match the newer, more specific one. This means you register general stubs first, then override specific cases afterward — the reverse of what feels intuitive to some people coming from "first match wins" systems:

```js
// General case first
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests/*', { fixture: 'leave-default.json' }).as('getDefault');

// Specific override, registered SECOND — takes priority for matching requests
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests/99', { statusCode: 404 }).as('getMissing');

cy.visit('/leave/1');   // uses leave-default.json
cy.visit('/leave/99');  // uses the 404 override
```

## Passing through selectively based on request content

```js
cy.intercept('POST', '/api/leave-requests', (req) => {
  if (req.body.type === 'unpaid') {
    req.reply({ statusCode: 403, body: { error: 'Unpaid leave requires manager pre-approval' } });
  } else {
    req.continue();   // let everything else hit the real backend
  }
}).as('createLeave');
```

## The cy.wait('@alias') return value, in full

This is worth having as a complete reference, since it's used constantly:

```js
cy.wait('@getLeave').then((interception) => {
  expect(interception.request.method).to.eq('GET');
  expect(interception.request.url).to.include('status=pending');
  expect(interception.request.headers['authorization']).to.include('Bearer');

  expect(interception.response.statusCode).to.eq(200);
  expect(interception.response.body).to.have.length(5);
  expect(interception.response.headers['content-type']).to.include('application/json');
});

// Shorthand accessors on the same object
cy.wait('@getLeave').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);
cy.wait('@getLeave').its('request.body').should('deep.include', { status: 'pending' });
```

## Asserting call counts and inspecting all calls

```js
cy.get('@getLeave.all').should('have.length', 1);          // exactly one call so far, without waiting
cy.wait('@getLeave');
cy.wait('@getLeave');                                       // waits for a SECOND call
cy.get('@getLeave.all').should('have.length', 2);

// Asserting a call did NOT happen — a real, useful negative assertion
cy.get('[data-cy=cancel]').click();
cy.get('@createLeave.all').should('have.length', 0);
```

## Waiting on multiple requests at once

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees').as('getEmployees');
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/departments').as('getDepartments');
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-types').as('getLeaveTypes');

cy.visit('/leave/new');

cy.wait(['@getEmployees', '@getDepartments', '@getLeaveTypes']);
// all three must complete before proceeding
```

This is the correct alternative to guessing a delay when a page fires several parallel requests on load.

## Aliasing convention as a reusable custom command

A pattern seen constantly in real projects — bundling related intercepts (from Part 2.5):

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('interceptLeaveApi', () => {
  cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests*').as('getLeave');
  cy.intercept('POST', '/api/leave-requests').as('createLeave');
  cy.intercept('PUT', '/api/leave-requests/*').as('updateLeave');
  cy.intercept('DELETE', '/api/leave-requests/*').as('deleteLeave');
});

beforeEach(() => {
  cy.interceptLeaveApi();
  cy.loginAs('employee');
  cy.visit('/leave');
  cy.wait('@getLeave');
});
```

## Asserting UI reacts correctly to loading, empty, and error states — the full picture

Bringing several threads together into one realistic sequence:

```js
describe('Leave list states', () => {
  it('shows loading, then data', () => {
    cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', (req) => {
      req.on('response', (res) => res.setDelay(500));
    }).as('getLeave');

    cy.visit('/leave');
    cy.get('[data-cy=skeleton]').should('be.visible');
    cy.wait('@getLeave');
    cy.get('[data-cy=skeleton]').should('not.exist');
    cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]').should('have.length.greaterThan', 0);
  });

  it('shows an empty state', () => {
    cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', []).as('getLeave');
    cy.visit('/leave');
    cy.wait('@getLeave');
    cy.get('[data-cy=empty-state]').should('be.visible');
  });

  it('shows an error state with retry', () => {
    cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', { statusCode: 500 }).as('getLeaveError');
    cy.visit('/leave');
    cy.wait('@getLeaveError');
    cy.get('[data-cy=error-banner]').should('be.visible');

    cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', { fixture: 'leave-requests.json' }).as('getLeaveRetry');
    cy.get('[data-cy=retry-btn]').click();
    cy.wait('@getLeaveRetry');
    cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]').should('have.length.greaterThan', 0);
  });
});
```

That last test demonstrates re-registering an intercept mid-test to change behavior on the retry — a genuinely useful pattern for testing recovery flows.

## GraphQL — a special mention

`cy.intercept()` handles GraphQL fine, but since most GraphQL traffic goes through a single POST /graphql endpoint, you match on the operation name inside the body instead of the URL:

```js
cy.intercept('POST', '/graphql', (req) => {
  if (req.body.operationName === 'GetLeaveRequests') {
    req.alias = 'getLeaveRequests';
  }
  if (req.body.operationName === 'CreateLeaveRequest') {
    req.alias = 'createLeaveRequest';
  }
});

cy.wait('@getLeaveRequests');
```

The cypress-graphql-mock and similar plugins wrap this pattern more conveniently, but the underlying mechanism is exactly this body-inspection technique.

## Comparison

```js
// Cypress — named alias, wait by name
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests').as('getLeave');
cy.wait('@getLeave');

// Playwright — route interception + a predicate-based wait
await page.route('**/api/leave-requests', (route) => {
  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify(mockData) });
});
await page.waitForResponse((res) => res.url().includes('/api/leave-requests'));
```

Both tools are genuinely first-class here — this is not an area of clear advantage for either. Playwright's page.route() + route.fulfill()/route.continue()/route.abort() maps almost one-to-one onto Cypress's stub/continue/force-error trio. The real difference, echoing Part 0.3, is ergonomics of waiting: Cypress's named alias (`cy.wait('@getLeave')`) is more compact and arguably more readable at a glance than Playwright's predicate-based waitForResponse(), but Playwright's predicate form is more flexible for matching on things a simple URL pattern can't express cleanly.

```python
# Selenium — no native interception; requires an external proxy
# (BrowserMob Proxy, mitmproxy) configured into the WebDriver session
proxy = Proxy()
proxy.proxy_type = ProxyType.MANUAL
# ... substantial setup to route traffic through a MITM proxy and inspect/modify it
```

Selenium has no equivalent whatsoever — network interception requires bolting on a separate proxy tool, configuring the browser to route through it, and inspecting traffic at the HTTP layer rather than the JS layer. This is meaningfully more setup and less precise than either Cypress's or Playwright's in-process interception, and it's one of the clearest, most practical reasons real teams move off Selenium for anything involving network-dependent UI testing.

The static-data half of the fixture/intercept pairing, and the organizational discipline that keeps a large fixtures folder maintainable.

## The basic mechanics, in full

```js
cy.fixture('employees').then((employees) => {
  expect(employees).to.have.length(10);
});

cy.fixture('employees.json');        // extension optional, .json assumed by default
```
`cy.fixture('users/admin')`;           // subfolders — resolves fixtures/users/admin.json
`cy.fixture('logo.png')`;              // binary — see 3.7's upload chapter

```js
cy.fixture('report.pdf', { encoding: null });   // raw Buffer, not a string
cy.fixture('employees', { timeout: 10000 });    // custom timeout, rarely needed
```

`cy.fixture()` is a parent command — it starts a fresh chain and doesn't depend on a prior subject, matching the categorization from Part 2.3.

## Where fixtures actually get used — three distinct roles

- **Role 1** — mock response bodies for cy.intercept() (5.1's primary use, and the most common in practice):

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees', { fixture: 'employees.json' });
```

- **Role 2** — input data for driving a test directly:

```js
cy.fixture('new-employee').then((employee) => {
  cy.get('[data-cy=first-name]').type(employee.firstName);
  cy.get('[data-cy=last-name]').type(employee.lastName);
  cy.get('[data-cy=department]').select(employee.department);
});
```

- **Role 3** — expected-value data for assertions, keeping "what should be true" out of the spec file and in a maintained data file:

```js
cy.fixture('expected-payroll-totals').then((expected) => {
  cy.get('[data-cy=total-gross]').should('have.text', expected.totalGross);
  cy.get('[data-cy=total-tax]').should('have.text', expected.totalTax);
});
```

## Organizing a fixtures folder that doesn't collapse under its own weight

A flat fixtures/ directory with 40 loosely-named JSON files is a maintenance problem waiting to happen. The convention that scales:

```text
cypress/fixtures/
├── employees/
│   ├── list.json              ← GET /api/employees response
│   ├── single.json            ← GET /api/employees/:id response
│   ├── new-valid.json         ← input data, valid case
│   └── new-invalid.json       ← input data, deliberately invalid
├── leave-requests/
│   ├── pending.json
│   ├── approved.json
│   ├── empty.json              ← []  — for empty-state tests
│   └── error-response.json     ← { "error": "..." }
├── payroll/
│   └── october-2026.json
└── users.json                  ← shared credentials, flat because it's used everywhere

cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees', { fixture: 'employees/list.json' });
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', { fixture: 'leave-requests/pending.json' });
```

## Dynamic modification of fixture data before use

Fixtures are static files, but nothing stops you from mutating the loaded object before handing it to `cy.intercept()` — this is how you keep one canonical fixture file while still covering variations:

```js
cy.fixture('leave-requests/pending.json').then((baseData) => {
  const modified = baseData.map((req) => ({ ...req, status: 'Approved' }));
  cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', modified).as('getLeave');
});

// Injecting a dynamic value a static file can't contain
cy.fixture('employees/new-valid.json').then((employee) => {
  const unique = { ...employee, email: `test.${Date.now()}@bizlevate.test` };
  cy.intercept('POST', '/api/employees', { statusCode: 201, body: unique }).as('createEmployee');
});
```

## Combining several fixtures for one intercept

```js
cy.fixture('employees/list.json').then((employees) => {
  cy.fixture('departments.json').then((departments) => {
    const merged = employees.map((e) => ({
      ...e,
      departmentName: departments.find((d) => d.id === e.departmentId)?.name,
    }));
    cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees', merged).as('getEmployees');
  });
});
```

For anything nesting more than two levels deep like this, aliasing each fixture first and combining in a single .then() (Part 2.5's flattening pattern) reads considerably better:

```js
cy.fixture('employees/list.json').as('employees');
cy.fixture('departments.json').as('departments');

cy.then(function () {
  const merged = this.employees.map((e) => ({
    ...e,
    departmentName: this.departments.find((d) => d.id === e.departmentId)?.name,
  }));
  cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees', merged).as('getEmployees');
});
```

## Cypress.env() vs fixtures — a distinction worth being precise about

These are frequently confused because both feel like "external test data," but they serve genuinely different purposes:

| | `Cypress.env()` / `cypress.env.json` | Fixtures |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Configuration and secrets | Test/mock data |
| Shape | Flat key-value | Arbitrary JSON, CSV, binary |
| Typical content | URLs, credentials, feature flags | API response bodies, form inputs |
| Committed to git? | Never (it's secrets) | Yes, normally |

```js
// Configuration — belongs in env
Cypress.env('apiUrl');
Cypress.env('adminPassword');

// Data — belongs in a fixture
cy.fixture('leave-requests/pending.json');
```

A credential used to log in is env data; a list of leave requests used to populate a mocked API response is fixture data. Mixing these up — putting API mock bodies in cypress.env.json, or committing real credentials into a fixture file — is a recurring beginner mistake worth calling out explicitly.

## Generating large or varied fixture data programmatically

For data too repetitive to hand-write, generate it once via a script rather than typing out fifty near-identical JSON objects:

```js
// scripts/generate-fixtures.js — run manually, output committed to fixtures/
const { faker } = require('@faker-js/faker');
const fs = require('fs');

const employees = Array.from({ length: 50 }, (_, i) => ({
  id: i + 1,
  name: faker.person.fullName(),
  email: faker.internet.email(),
  department: faker.helpers.arrayElement(['Engineering', 'HR', 'Finance', 'Sales']),
  joinDate: faker.date.past({ years: 3 }).toISOString().split('T')[0],
}));

fs.writeFileSync('cypress/fixtures/employees/large-list.json', JSON.stringify(employees, null, 2));
```

Note this generation happens outside the test run, as a one-time (or periodically re-run) script — not inside setupNodeEvents on every test run, which would make fixture content non-deterministic across runs and defeat the point of having a fixture at all. If you need genuinely fresh random data per test run, that's a `cy.task()` use case instead (Part 1.9), not a fixture — fixtures are meant to be static and version-controlled.

## Cypress.env()-driven fixture selection — environment-aware fixtures

```js
const env = Cypress.env('environment') || 'local';
cy.fixture(`leave-requests/${env}/pending.json`).then((data) => {
  cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', data);
});
```

Useful when staging and local mock data genuinely need to differ (different seeded IDs, different date ranges) — though this is a pattern to reach for only when actually necessary, since it adds a layer of indirection to every fixture lookup.

## Fixtures and TypeScript

From Part 1.8, typing fixture contents pays off directly here:

```js
import type { Employee } from '../support/types';

cy.fixture<Employee[]>('employees/list.json').then((employees) => {
  expect(employees[0].department).to.eq('Engineering');   // autocompleted, type-checked
});
```

## Comparison

```js
// Cypress — a bundled, first-class file-loading command
cy.fixture('employees.json').then((data) => { ... });

// Playwright — no dedicated fixture-loading command; use Node's fs directly,
// since test code already runs in Node
import employees from '../fixtures/employees.json';
// or
const employees = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('fixtures/employees.json', 'utf-8'));
```

This is a case where Cypress's in-browser execution model — normally a source of friction (Part 0.5, Part 1.9's `cy.task()`) — actually produces a nicer, purpose-built API. Because Playwright test code runs in Node already, it needs no special "fixture loading" command at all; a plain import or readFileSync does the job, which is simpler but also means there's no equivalent single verb that signals "this is test data" the way `cy.fixture()` does. (Note: Playwright separately uses the word "fixture" for its dependency-injection test-setup mechanism — a completely different concept from a static data file, and a naming collision worth being aware of if you're reading Playwright docs alongside this manual.)
Selenium has no concept of fixtures at all; test data loading is whatever your language's file-reading facilities and your test framework's data-provider mechanism (@DataProvider in TestNG, pytest.fixture in pytest — again, a different "fixture" than either JS tool's usage) happen to offer.

A consolidated, cross-referencing pass over the three alias kinds — this chapter exists to make Part 2.5's material directly applicable in networking contexts, and to cover the parts specifically relevant once `cy.intercept()` is in the picture.

## Why this gets its own chapter here

Part 2.5 introduced the three kinds of alias in general terms. Now that `cy.intercept()` and `cy.fixture()` are fully in view, it's worth returning to aliases specifically as the connective tissue between network mocking and test assertions — this is genuinely where aliases do their most important work in a real suite.

## Network aliases — the complete picture, brought together

```js
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests').as('getLeave');
cy.intercept('POST', '/api/leave-requests').as('createLeave');
cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', { fixture: 'leave-requests/pending.json' }).as('getLeaveMocked');
```

The alias namespace for network intercepts is entirely separate from element and data aliases — `cy.get('@getLeave')` on a network alias returns interception data, not a DOM element, even though the syntax is identical. Cypress determines which kind of alias it is by what was aliased in the first place, not by anything in the retrieval syntax itself.

```js
cy.wait('@getLeave');                                    // wait for completion
cy.get('@getLeave');                                      // get the interception WITHOUT waiting again
cy.get('@getLeave.all');                                  // all calls so far
cy.get('@getLeave.1');                                    // the second call (0-indexed)
cy.wait('@getLeave').its('response.body').as('leaveData'); // chain into a DATA alias
```

That last line is worth pausing on — it demonstrates aliases composing across categories. @getLeave is a network alias; .its('response.body') extracts data from it; .as('leaveData') then creates a new, separate data alias from that extracted value. From that point forward, `cy.get('@leaveData')` yields the plain JavaScript value, not interception metadata.

```js
cy.wait('@getLeave').its('response.body').as('leaveData');

cy.get('@leaveData').then((leave) => {
  cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]').should('have.length', leave.length);
  cy.contains('[data-cy=leave-row]', leave[0].employeeName).should('be.visible');
});
```

## A realistic end-to-end pattern combining all three alias kinds

```js
describe('Leave approval flow', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    cy.fixture('leave-requests/pending.json').as('pendingRequests');      // DATA alias

    cy.get('@pendingRequests').then((requests) => {
      cy.intercept('GET', '/api/leave-requests', requests).as('getLeave'); // NETWORK alias
    });

    cy.intercept('PUT', '/api/leave-requests/*/approve').as('approveLeave'); // NETWORK alias

    cy.loginAs('manager');
    cy.visit('/leave/approvals');
    cy.wait('@getLeave');

    cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]').as('leaveRows');                         // ELEMENT alias
  });

  it('approves the first pending request', function () {
    cy.get('@leaveRows').should('have.length', this.pendingRequests.length);

    cy.get('@leaveRows').first().find('[data-cy=approve-btn]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=confirm]').click();

    cy.wait('@approveLeave').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);
    cy.contains('[data-cy=toast]', 'Leave approved').should('be.visible');
  });
});
```

This single beforeEach sets up all three kinds in sequence — data from a fixture, a network stub built from that data, and an element alias for the rendered rows — and the test body reads cleanly because none of the underlying complexity leaks into it.

## Waiting on an alias and then acting on a related element alias

A genuinely common real pattern: wait for a request to settle, then interact with the DOM it caused to render, using aliases to avoid re-querying:

```js
it('refreshes the row after approval without a full reload', () => {
  cy.get('@leaveRows').first().find('[data-cy=approve-btn]').click();
  cy.wait('@approveLeave');

  // The alias re-queries automatically (Part 2.5) — this reflects the POST-approval DOM
  cy.get('@leaveRows').first().find('[data-cy=status]').should('have.text', 'Approved');
});
```

This relies on the element-alias re-query behavior from Part 2.5 — @leaveRows isn't a frozen snapshot from beforeEach, it's a stored query that re-runs, so it correctly reflects the DOM after the approval re-renders that row.

## Aliasing intercepts dynamically, based on runtime data

```js
cy.fixture('employees/list.json').then((employees) => {
  employees.forEach((emp) => {
    cy.intercept('GET', `/api/employees/${emp.id}`, emp).as(`employee${emp.id}`);
  });
});

cy.get('[data-cy=employee-select]').select('123');
cy.wait('@employee123');
```

Dynamically-generated alias names like this are a legitimate pattern for data-driven scenarios (fuller treatment in Part 9.14), though they trade off some readability — a hardcoded @employee123 scattered through assertions is less self-documenting than a fixed alias name, so use this only when the dynamism genuinely earns its keep.

## The gotcha that specifically bites once intercepts and data aliases mix

```js
// ❌ A classic ordering bug: the intercept alias and the fixture alias
// look similar but are NOT interchangeable
cy.fixture('leave-requests/pending.json').as('getLeave');    // this is a DATA alias
```
`cy.wait('@getLeave')`;   // ERROR — `cy.wait()` only works on NETWORK aliases

Naming a fixture alias the same way you'd name a network alias is a real source of confusion, especially on a team where both patterns are common. A practical convention that avoids this entirely: prefix or suffix data aliases distinctly from network aliases — @leaveFixture / @getLeave, or @leaveData / @leaveRequest — so the alias name itself signals which of Part 2.5's three categories it belongs to.

## Comparison

Nothing new to add here beyond Part 2.5's comparison — Playwright's plain-variable model and Selenium's total absence of an alias concept apply identically once network mocking enters the picture. The one Cypress-specific point worth restating in this context: the alias system is what makes the fixture/intercept/DOM triad in the combined example above readable as a single coherent flow, rather than the deeply nested .then() pyramid it would otherwise require — which is a genuine, distinctly-Cypress ergonomic win once a test is coordinating all three data sources at once.
